enes they see these  numbers as pretty positive they think  it's a good first step and they are of  course moving towards making it work  more effectively as the lockdown is  lifted but there's still a missing link  in this whole test system and that's the  app we were told that that was going to  be crucial to helping people know who  they'd come into contact with we're now  told that when lockdown is lifted so  later on this year that will become more  crucial because when people start to  move around more and they come into  contact with more people then the app  will be useful in helping people to  track who they may have come into  contact with over time at the moment  because most people are in  down and therefore not moving that much  the contact tracing system can be done  by humans at the end of a telephone one  interesting thing to note from the Isle  of Wight testing that's been done the  results actually show that people are  much more inclined to listen if it's a  person telling them they need to self  isolate rather than an app so that may  well have guided the government's  thinking on how to proceed with this  test track and trace system yeah that's  an interesting finding isn't it just on  the social distancing Kate we just heard  Nicola Sturgeon restating the argument  for the two meter rule to be continued  with we'll be talking to Ian Devlin  Smith Tori grandi former Conservative  Party leader in the next few minutes he  will argue the opposite that one metre  is good enough for most of the world not  good enough for the UK will help  business where are we heading on that  well he's not alone in Duncan Smith is  one of a number of very senior backbench  Conservative MPs and actually there are  ministers too who are asking Boris  Johnson to do this as quickly as  possible and they want it to happen  because there are a number of bars and  restaurants other venues where people  mix in close proximity who say that they  just can't operate they won't be  financially viable if the 2 meter  guideline is stuck to by the government  it's also a problem for schools because  trying to get all the pupils crammed  into classrooms and keeping them apart  is proving problematic there's not  enough space we know actually that  Downing Street and Boris Johnson also  keen to lift this restriction but they  will only do so when they know that the  infection rate in the community is low  enough to do it without a huge risk of  the virus getting out of control again  the problem is at the moment we don't  know what that level has to be so we've  got no guidance as to when that might  happen  but what we do know is that scientists  have said 2 metres is safe at the moment  because it reduces the risk from the  droplets from your mouth you can reduce  that distance to 1 metre but you might  need to use other things like a mask you  may need to sit next to somebody rather  than across from them if for example you  were in a restaurant and the government  is sending a message that they are  looking at this as a matter of urgency  here is a government minister speaking  to us a little bit earlier on today  everybody should maintain to meet the  social distancing are at this point this  is the clear guidance that we've issued  to everybody it's what we want people to  observe we would love to make progress  on this point but we're not yet  do that you know clearly we do take into  account our own scientific and they've  been very clear that this is what they  recommend at the end of the day though  this will be a political decision the  scientists will advise Boris Johnson but  he will be the man who has to decide  when it is the right time to reduce that  limit and there will be implications  from that decision of course and this is  still a very new virus that lots of  people don't really fully understand so  a difficult time for Boris Johnson but  he is being pushed by all sides to make  that decision sooner rather than later
 

